    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you go a bit deeper into the downstream effects? Like, how does the decrease in angiotensin II specifically lead to that reduction in aldosterone, and what's the actual impact on the kidneys in terms of electrolyte balance?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Angiotensin II normally stimulates the adrenal cortex to release aldosterone. With ACE inhibitors decreasing angiotensin II levels, this stimulating signal to the adrenal glands is diminished, thus lowering aldosterone secretion. Lower aldosterone levels lead to reduced sodium reabsorption (and potassium excretion) in the distal nephron of the kidneys. This increased sodium and water loss in the urine correlates with the desired overall reduction in extracellular fluid volume and blood pressure.",
      "source": "target",
