    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Okay, I think I follow. So if aldosterone is low, potassium isn't being excreted as much, right? Can you explain how that link works at the cellular level in the nephron? I'm trying to visualize the actual exchange happening.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Aldosterone primarily acts on principal cells in the collecting duct, promoting the synthesis and insertion of ENaC channels, which actively reabsorb sodium ($\\text{Na}^{+}$) from the filtrate. Simultaneously, it stimulates the basolateral $\\text{Na}^{+}/\\text{K}^{+}$-ATPase pump to drive potassium ($\\text{K}^{+}$) into the cell. This creates a favorable electrochemical gradient that drives potassium secretion into the urine. When aldosterone levels drop, this activity decreases, resulting in less $\\text{K}^{+}$ being secreted and thus decreased potassium excretion.",
      "source": "target",
